In this lesson we'll look at factorials and permutations, two shortcuts that make counting arrangements way easier. First, factorial notation. When you see the exclamation mark after a number, it just means keep multiplying down to one. So factorial of five is five times four times three times two times one, which gives one hundred and twenty. Quick note here: by convention, factorial of zero is defined as one, and that's a small but important rule you'll use a lot. Next, permutations. A permutation is just an ordered arrangement. If you want to know how many ways you can line up some items chosen from a bigger group, with no repeats allowed, you use the permutation formula. For example, if you pick three things in order from ten, you multiply ten by nine by eight, since each choice uses up an item. That gives seven hundred and twenty orderings. Finally, here's a neat connection to probability. If every arrangement is equally likely, the chance of landing on any single specific one is just one divided by the total number of arrangements. So for our ten items arranged in order, the probability of one exact arrangement is one over ten factorial. Now let's see it in action.